<article class="container pt-10 pb-5">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <h1 class="pb-3">Network and edge</h1>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">From fibre to the office and the house</h2>
            <p>
                My 2 Gbit/s fibre connection comes into a NanoPi R6S running FriendlyWrt. The WAN connects at 2.5 GbE, and the two LAN ports feed the office and house.
                The office uses three 2.5 GbE switches and connects the Proxmox cluster and development workstations. The house uses four gigabit switches and three Tenda Nova Wi-Fi mesh nodes in bridge mode.
            </p>
            <pre class="bg-light rounded-3 p-3"><code>2 Gbit/s fibre
└── NanoPi R6S / FriendlyWrt (2.5 GbE WAN)
    ├── Office (2.5 GbE LAN)
    │   └── 3 × 2.5 GbE switches
    └── House (1 GbE LAN)
        ├── 4 × 1 GbE switches
        └── 3 × Wi-Fi mesh nodes in bridge mode</code></pre>
            <p>
                The two wired branches are part of the same LAN bridge. FriendlyWrt provides the default gateway, firewall and DHCP,
                and DHCP hands out both Pi-hole addresses. The house uplink is gigabit, so traffic from that branch to the internet or office shares that link.
                The diagram groups switches and access points by branch.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Maintaining FriendlyWrt on the NanoPi R6S</h2>
            <p>
                I use FriendlyElec’s Docker-capable FriendlyWrt build because the router also runs DNS and the reverse proxy.
                I record the running firmware, driver and packages before upgrading. The commands below use the R6S interface names from my setup.
                The <a href="https://wiki.friendlyelec.com/wiki/index.php/NanoPi_R6S">NanoPi R6S wiki</a> covers port mapping, eMMC Tools and recovery.
                Firmware and checksums are available from <a href="https://github.com/friendlyarm/Actions-FriendlyWrt/releases">FriendlyWrt releases</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">ubus call system board
uname -a
ip -br link
ethtool eth2
ethtool -i eth2
apk info &gt; /tmp/friendlywrt-packages.txt
sysupgrade -b /tmp/friendlywrt-config.tar.gz
tar -tzf /tmp/friendlywrt-config.tar.gz</code></pre>
            <p>
                I copy the archive and package list off the router, back up the Docker stacks and their persistent data separately,
                and keep a bootable recovery microSD card handy. The <code>sysupgrade -b</code> command creates the configuration archive.
                I compare the downloaded image’s SHA-256 with the value published for that exact release before uploading it.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">sha256sum ./friendlywrt-r6s-image.tgz</code></pre>
            <p>
                For the eMMC upgrade I use <strong>System → eMMC Tools</strong> in LuCI with the correct FriendlyElec archive for the R6S and its partition layout.
                For a compatible minor upgrade the vendor tool offers a data-preserving option. My last upgrade still booted with factory settings,
                so I reapplied the network and service settings selectively and remounted the Docker data. After boot I check the kernel and Ethernet driver again,
                then test WAN routing, both DNS servers and HTTPS.
            </p>
            <p>
                FriendlyWrt 25.12 uses <code>apk</code>. I use <code>apk update</code> to refresh the package index and
                <code>apk list --upgradable</code> to inspect it. For system-wide updates I use a compatible firmware image, which supplies a matched kernel, drivers and packages.
                In particular, I update the Realtek 2.5 GbE driver with the firmware. See the
                <a href="https://openwrt.org/docs/guide-user/additional-software/apk">OpenWrt APK guide</a>.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Two Pi-hole DNS servers</h2>
            <p>
                My primary Pi-hole runs in Docker on the NanoPi, with an Unbound container on the same Docker bridge. My secondary runs natively on a Raspberry Pi 3B,
                with its own Unbound service on loopback. Each Pi-hole filters requests and sends public lookups to its own recursive resolver.
                Clients can use either Pi-hole, so I keep local records and filtering policy in sync by hand and update one server at a time.
                The addresses and hostnames in the commands below are examples to adapt to your LAN.
            </p>

            <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Primary Pi-hole on the NanoPi</h3>
            <p>
                I run this stack from <code>/opt/stacks/pihole</code>. This example pins the images to versions used in the lab.
                Pi-hole publishes DNS on TCP and UDP port 53 and its dashboard on port 8088, leaving ports 80 and 443 for the proxy.
                Unbound is reachable from Pi-hole on the Docker bridge. Pick a bridge subnet that does not overlap your LAN or VPN,
                and set <code>PIHOLE_PASSWORD</code> in a private <code>.env</code> file next to <code>docker-compose.yml</code>.
                See the <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/docker/">Pi-hole Docker guide</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-yaml">services:
  pihole:
    image: pihole/pihole:2026.11.0
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "192.168.50.1:53:53/tcp"
      - "192.168.50.1:53:53/udp"
      - "192.168.50.1:8088:80/tcp"
    environment:
      TZ: Europe/London
      FTLCONF_webserver_api_password: "${PIHOLE_PASSWORD:?Set PIHOLE_PASSWORD in .env}"
      FTLCONF_dns_listeningMode: ALL
      FTLCONF_dns_upstreams: "172.30.53.2#53"
    volumes:
      - ./etc-pihole:/etc/pihole
    networks:
      dns:
    depends_on:
      - unbound
  unbound:
    image: klutchell/unbound:v1.26.2-20261119
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks:
      dns:
        ipv4_address: 172.30.53.2
networks:
  dns:
    ipam:
      config:
        - subnet: 172.30.53.0/24</code></pre>
            <p>
                The router’s LAN address needs TCP and UDP port 53 available before the stack starts.
                If dnsmasq currently serves DNS there, move its DNS listener or disable its DNS function while keeping DHCP enabled.
                Once both Pi-holes answer, set DHCP option <code>6,192.168.50.1,192.168.50.16</code> on the LAN in LuCI.
                Renew a client’s lease and check that it received both servers.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">cd /opt/stacks/pihole
chmod 600 .env
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker compose exec pihole pihole status</code></pre>
            <p>
                For a Docker update I export the Pi-hole settings with Teleporter and save the Compose file and persistent data off the router.
                Then I change the pinned tag for the component I’m updating. The same pull and recreate sequence works for the Unbound service
                by using <code>unbound</code> as the service name. I check DNS after each change.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">docker compose config --quiet
docker compose pull pihole
docker compose up -d --no-deps pihole
docker compose exec pihole pihole version
docker compose logs --tail=30 pihole</code></pre>

            <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Secondary Pi-hole on the Raspberry Pi</h3>
            <p>
                Give the Pi a stable LAN address, such as <code>192.168.50.16</code>. I use the native installer on this host and keep DHCP on FriendlyWrt.
                I install Unbound with APT, then save the loopback configuration from the Unbound guide as <code>/etc/unbound/unbound.conf.d/pi-hole.conf</code>
                before running the configuration check below.
                See the <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/main/basic-install/">Pi-hole installation guide</a> and the
                <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/guides/dns/unbound/">Unbound configuration guide</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt update
sudo apt install unbound dnsutils

# After saving the Unbound configuration from the guide
sudo unbound-checkconf
sudo systemctl enable --now unbound
sudo systemctl restart unbound
dig @@127.0.0.1 -p 5335 example.org

curl -fsSL https://install.pi-hole.net -o basic-install.sh
less basic-install.sh
sudo bash basic-install.sh
sudo pihole-FTL --config dns.upstreams '["127.0.0.1#5335"]'
sudo systemctl restart pihole-FTL
sudo pihole status</code></pre>
            <p>
                After exporting the secondary Pi-hole settings, I use the native updater for Pi-hole and the Debian package manager for Unbound.
                See the <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/main/pihole-command/#update">Pi-hole update command</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">sudo pihole -up
sudo apt update
sudo apt install --only-upgrade unbound
sudo systemctl is-active pihole-FTL unbound</code></pre>
            <p>
                From another LAN machine, I query both DNS servers directly. In this example the local name points to the reverse proxy on the NanoPi.
                Add that same local record to each Pi-hole before testing the local lookup.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">for dns in 192.168.50.1 192.168.50.16; do
    dig "@@$dns" example.org
    dig "@@$dns" +tcp example.org
    dig "@@$dns" app.home.arpa +short
done</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Nginx Proxy Manager on the NanoPi</h2>
            <p>
                Nginx Proxy Manager (NPM) gives my internal services usable names and terminates public HTTPS for the CodeBuildDeploy dev sites.
                It runs in host-network mode so it can reach the private Istio frontend in Azure Kubernetes Service over the router’s WireGuard tunnel.
                The proxy uses ports 80 and 443, and I use port 81 for LAN administration in this example. Keep that admin port restricted to the LAN.
                See the <a href="https://nginxproxymanager.com/setup/">Nginx Proxy Manager setup guide</a> and
                <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/host/">Docker host networking</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                I keep a separate Compose stack under <code>/opt/stacks/npm</code>. The <code>data</code> directory contains the configuration and SQLite database,
                and <code>letsencrypt</code> stores certificate material.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-yaml">services:
  npm:
    image: jc21/nginx-proxy-manager:2.17.1
    network_mode: host
    restart: unless-stopped
    volumes:
      - ./data:/data
      - ./letsencrypt:/etc/letsencrypt</code></pre>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">cd /opt/stacks/npm
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker compose exec npm nginx -t</code></pre>
            <p>
                In the admin UI, I create a proxy host, enter its hostname and backend address, and select the certificate on the SSL tab.
                For the dev sites I select HTTPS and port 443 at the private upstream. In the Advanced tab I set the upstream TLS server name and enable certificate verification.
                Replace <code>dev.example.org</code> with the name on the upstream certificate for that proxy host. See the
                <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_ssl_name">Nginx upstream TLS directives</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-nginx">proxy_ssl_server_name on;
proxy_ssl_name dev.example.org;
proxy_ssl_verify on;
proxy_ssl_verify_depth 3;
proxy_ssl_trusted_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt;</code></pre>
            <p>
                Before upgrading, I read the release notes and back up the Compose file and both data directories with NPM stopped, so the SQLite database
                and certificate files can be restored together. I run these commands as the stack administrator and copy the backup off the router.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">cd /opt/stacks/npm
umask 077
backup="$HOME/npm-before-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).tar.gz"
docker compose stop npm
tar -czf "$backup" docker-compose.yml data letsencrypt
tar -tzf "$backup"</code></pre>
            <p>
                Once the backup checks out, I change the pinned image tag, pull it and recreate the service.
                If the backup fails, I bring the existing service back with <code>docker compose start npm</code> and resolve the backup issue before upgrading.
                See the <a href="https://nginxproxymanager.com/upgrading/">Nginx Proxy Manager upgrade guide</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">docker compose config --quiet
docker compose pull npm
docker compose up -d --no-deps npm
docker compose exec npm nginx -t
docker compose logs --tail=30 npm

# From a LAN client, test HTTPS at the proxy using the real site name
curl --fail --silent --show-error --output /dev/null \
    --resolve dev.example.org:443:192.168.50.1 https://dev.example.org/</code></pre>
            <p>
                I also repeat the HTTPS check from outside the home network for public sites. That exercises public DNS, the edge certificate,
                the WireGuard path and the application response together.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
